This directory builds ONE shared libgnu.a for every GNU package in
sys/src/ape/cmd that needs gnulib.

We have tried to reduce the dependency on gnulib as much as possible by
re-implementing a lot of the API using thin shims in libap. Some things
are bigger and weirder and those things are probably better to keep in a
separate library -- that is what this is.


How it is put together
----------------------

Sources come from sys/src/external/gnulib, a single consolidated tree
produced by that directory's import.sh from the gnulib copies vendored
inside the individual GNU packages. Priority is by gnulib vintage,
newest first:

    coreutils (2026) > m4 (2026) > diffutils (2025) > ggrep (2025)
    > patch (2025) > gtar (2023) > gsed (2022) > bison (2021)

coreutils is the base -- it has the newest gnulib and the widest module
coverage -- so it supplies config-common.h and decides the header set
that everything compiles against. Later trees only fill in modules
coreutils does not ship (bitset, fstrcmp, timevar, spawn-pipe and
friends).

This replaced an arrangement where each package compiled its own gnulib
objects and appended them to a shared archive with 'ar vu'. Which copy of
a module ended up in the archive then depended on build order, and stale
members outlived the lists that put them there.


config-common.h and the per-package config.h
--------------------------------------------

The shared tree holds config-common.h, not config.h: the platform half
of the answers, meaning the HAVE_* probes, the type sizes and the APExp
additions appended by import.sh.

Package identity is deliberately not in it. The file began as coreutils'
config.h, so it said PACKAGE "coreutils" and VERSION "9.11", and bison's
src reads VERSION, PACKAGE_STRING, PACKAGE_URL and PACKAGE_BUGREPORT for
its --version output. Left alone, bison compiled and linked cleanly and
then introduced itself as GNU coreutils 9.11. import.sh comments those
nine macros out.

So every build directory that links libgnu.a has its own small config.h:

    #include <config-common.h>
    #define PACKAGE       "bison"
    #define PACKAGE_STRING "GNU Bison 3.8"
    ...

found through -I. while config-common.h is found through -I$GNUSRC.
That is why the shared file was renamed: under the name config.h it
would shadow every one of them. This directory has such a config.h too,
holding no identity, because gnulib's own sources all open with
#include <config.h>.

The package's own config.h in its source tree stays unused. Those
predate the 2026 gnulib being linked, so their platform answers no
longer describe the objects.


Four rules for editing any of this
----------------------------------

1. Never add a module that libap already provides.

   libap.a and libgnu.a are both on the link line for every package, so a
   module present in both is a duplicate-symbol error, not a silent
   preference. The failure can be indirect: gnulib splits obstack into
   obstack.c and obstack_printf.c, while libap's string/obstack.c
   implements both halves in one object. Linking gnulib's obstack.o
   satisfied _obstack_begin, but the first reference to obstack_printf
   then dragged in libap's object, which redefined every _obstack_*
   symbol. Symptom:

       _obstack_begin: libap.a(obstack_printf): redefinition: _obstack_begin

   Already provided by libap, do not add: obstack, exitfail, the musl
   <stdio_ext.h> accessors behind freadahead, fseterr and fwriting, and
   the whole x*alloc family -- xmalloc, xalloc-die, xreallocarray,
   xnmalloc, xnrealloc, xcharalloc, xzalloc, x2realloc, x2nrealloc,
   xpalloc and the idx_t variants (ximalloc, xirealloc, xicalloc,
   xizalloc, ximemdup, ximemdup0).

2. Do not let a package rebuild a module that lives here.

   The same duplicate-symbol trap applies to a package's own private
   archive. diff's libdiff.a and sed's libsed.a existed only to hold
   version-etc and progname, which this library now supplies, so both
   were removed outright.

3. Watch for gnulib module renames when merging OFILES lists.

   The object list started as the union of what each package asked for,
   and those lists span gnulib vintages. When gnulib renames a module it
   keeps the function names, so both files land in the tree and both
   define the same symbols. 'ar' reports it while building the archive:

       duplicate text symbol: hash_pjw

   Three such pairs were removed. The legacy name is on the left:

       hash-pjw            -> hashcode-string2
       hash-triple-simple  -> hashcode-named-file
       canonicalize-lgpl   -> canonicalize   (alternatives, not a rename;
                                              canonicalize is the superset,
                                              adding canonicalize_filename_mode)

   Adding a module means checking that nothing already in OFILES defines
   its symbols under another file name.

4. Keep the -I order right in each package's mkfile.

       -I.               the package's own config.h
       -I<pkg>/src       only where the package has a header whose name
                         collides with one here. bison is the sole case:
                         bison/src/configmake.h is hand-edited to give
                         PKGDATADIR "/sys/lib/ape", and the gnulib
                         configmake.h has no PKGDATADIR at all, so
                         getting the wrong one is a compile error.
       -I$GNUSRC         the shared tree, ahead of the package's own copies
                         of the same module headers, so declarations match
                         the objects in libgnu.a. bison alone shares 203
                         header names with this tree.
       -I<pkg>/lib       last, for headers only the package provides.

   Getting the last two the wrong way round fails at link time with a
   missing symbol rather than at compile time, so it does not look like a
   header problem. bison hit it as

       _convM2D: is_basic_table: not defined

   because bison's 2021 mbchar.h has an inline reading is_basic_table[],
   a table the 2026 mbchar.c no longer defines.

5. version-etc is built per package, not shared.

   Nearly every gnulib module is identity-free and belongs in the shared
   archive. version-etc.c is the exception: emit_bug_reporting_address()
   compiles PACKAGE_BUGREPORT, PACKAGE_NAME and PACKAGE_URL straight
   into the object, and version_etc_arn() does the same with
   PACKAGE_PACKAGER. One shared copy would give every program the bug
   address of whichever package built it, and it would still compile and
   link, so nothing would catch it.

   It is therefore absent from OFILES. sed, m4 and diff each build it in
   their own directory with an explicit rule, against their own config.h
   but from the shared source, so it matches the headers everything else
   uses. bison and patch never call it.

   version-etc-fsf.c stays shared: it holds only the FSF copyright
   notice and names no package. Checked the rest of OFILES for the same
   problem and version-etc.c is the only case; backup-find.c looks like
   one but its VERSION is the VERSION_CONTROL environment variable.

   If a future module turns out to read PACKAGE_*, it belongs here for
   the same reason.

The mkfile deletes libgnu.a before re-archiving rather than using
'ar vu' in place, so dropping a module from OFILES actually removes it.
